WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.589
Picture a café with two branches that keep the same price list.

2
00:00:06.867 --> 00:00:16.516
The owner changes prices at the first branch, and texts the manager of the second: raise every price by 10 cents, and write today's date at the top.

3
00:00:16.767 --> 00:00:20.955
He reads the text the next morning, so his list carries a different date.

4
00:00:21.233 --> 00:00:25.072
Then her phone says: not delivered, and sends the text again.

5
00:00:25.072 --> 00:00:27.967
And the second branch raises its prices twice.

6
00:00:28.233 --> 00:00:30.488
Sending the instruction felt natural.

7
00:00:30.488 --> 00:00:32.621
It's short, and anyone can read it.

8
00:00:32.621 --> 00:00:37.619
But the second branch did the work again, at another time, and got another answer.

9
00:00:37.867 --> 00:00:41.180
Coffee at branch two is now the priciest in town.

10
00:00:41.667 --> 00:00:43.665
Databases face the same choice.

11
00:00:43.665 --> 00:00:51.398
In One leader, many followers, the leader wrote every change into a numbered log, and the followers applied it in order.

12
00:00:51.398 --> 00:00:53.783
But what does one entry actually say?

13
00:00:54.067 --> 00:00:56.058
Changes arrive as statements.

14
00:00:56.058 --> 00:01:01.550
A statement is one command in SQL, the language most databases take commands in.

15
00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:06.012
This one marks every payment in batch 42 as paid out to its shop.

16
00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:09.680
The simplest log writes the statement down.

17
00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:12.904
59 bytes, even if it changes 80,000 rows.

18
00:01:12.904 --> 00:01:14.555
A person can read it.

19
00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:21.186
MySQL, a popular free database, copied changes only this way until version 5.1.

20
00:01:21.186 --> 00:01:25.309
It's the text message: each follower runs it again.

21
00:01:25.567 --> 00:01:27.930
Let's test it with the lesson's demo.

22
00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:29.975
The leader runs five statements.

23
00:01:30.667 --> 00:01:36.561
Three add a payment, each with a reference code made from a random number, and the time from now.

24
00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:40.397
One adds a fee of 30 to every payment of shop m1.

25
00:01:40.397 --> 00:01:42.893
And one deletes shop m2's payment.

26
00:01:43.133 --> 00:01:47.825
Follower S runs each statement 350 milliseconds after the leader.

27
00:01:47.825 --> 00:01:53.600
Then, as can happen after a reconnect, the end of the log arrives a second time.

28
00:01:53.867 --> 00:01:56.646
Will the follower's table match the leader's?

29
00:01:56.646 --> 00:01:57.635
Pause and guess.

30
00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:02.258
Not one row matches.

31
00:02:02.258 --> 00:02:03.480
Look at payment 1.

32
00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:07.555
The reference code: the follower drew its own random number.

33
00:02:07.767 --> 00:02:12.765
The time: 350 milliseconds later, from the follower's own clock.

34
00:02:13.033 --> 00:02:18.416
And the fee: 60, not 30, because "add 30" arrived twice, and ran twice.

35
00:02:18.667 --> 00:02:20.058
It's the café again.

36
00:02:20.058 --> 00:02:21.937
The date at the top is now.

37
00:02:21.937 --> 00:02:24.233
The double price rise is the fee.

38
00:02:24.533 --> 00:02:26.965
And there are quieter ways to differ.

39
00:02:26.965 --> 00:02:29.921
Two clients add a payment at the same moment.

40
00:02:29.921 --> 00:02:37.609
The leader gives them ids 7 and 8, but the second becomes final first, so the follower runs it first, and gives it 7.

41
00:02:37.833 --> 00:02:40.480
Each follower did exactly what the log said.

42
00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:42.945
The log just told it to think for itself.

43
00:02:43.433 --> 00:02:45.176
Can the leader repair this?

44
00:02:45.176 --> 00:02:52.407
It can write its own values into each statement: the actual code and the actual time, instead of random and now.

45
00:02:52.667 --> 00:02:54.359
MySQL does some of this.

46
00:02:54.359 --> 00:02:57.745
Which difference goes away, and which one stays?

47
00:02:57.745 --> 00:02:58.873
Pause and guess.

48
00:03:02.133 --> 00:03:03.958
Codes and times now match.

49
00:03:03.958 --> 00:03:05.361
The fee is still 60.

50
00:03:05.361 --> 00:03:10.132
Writing in the leader's values doesn't make "add 30" safe to repeat.

51
00:03:10.367 --> 00:03:16.603
The rule underneath: a statement must give the same result every time it runs, wherever it runs.

52
00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:26.607
Durable execution met the same rule: a workflow reads the time and random numbers through the engine, so every replay makes the same choices.

53
00:03:26.607 --> 00:03:28.604
For SQL, nothing enforces it.

54
00:03:28.867 --> 00:03:31.449
So log what the statement did instead.

55
00:03:31.449 --> 00:03:36.411
The row log says: payment 1, fee 30, with the leader's own code and time.

56
00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:41.229
Statement 4 became two row entries, one for each row it changed.

57
00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:45.389
And the row copy matches the leader exactly, resend and all.

58
00:03:45.667 --> 00:03:49.625
So the rule to keep: a statement runs again on every follower.

59
00:03:49.625 --> 00:03:53.647
Unless something forces the same result, don't ship statements.

60
00:03:53.647 --> 00:03:54.860
Ship what they did.

61
00:03:55.433 --> 00:03:56.618
One quick check.

62
00:03:56.618 --> 00:04:02.985
A follower that was loaded from a copy receives: delete shop m2's payments, limit 100.

63
00:04:02.985 --> 00:04:06.094
Does it delete the same 100 as the leader?

64
00:04:06.094 --> 00:04:07.279
Pause and think.

65
00:04:10.467 --> 00:04:10.686
No.

66
00:04:10.686 --> 00:04:16.168
It deletes the first 100 it meets, and it stores its rows in another order.

67
00:04:16.168 --> 00:04:17.996
So it deletes other rows.
